net2.patch - a room of our own and the direct launch for the native build: the lines in files
other work owns.  docs/issues/native-rendezvous.md says what it is and what was run.

Made against tools/recomp/net/netdrv.cpp as it stood 2026-10-12 23:50 (unchanged on
2026-10-13 23:05) and tools/recomp/app/build.py of 2026-10-13 14:30; a dry run against
app/build.py of 22:09 applies cleanly.  The game runs of docs/issues/native-rendezvous.md
section 6 were made with exactly these lines (netdrv.cpp of a snapshot, patched).  Apply from tools/recomp:

    cd tools/recomp && patch -p1 --dry-run < net2/net2.patch && patch -p1 < net2/net2.patch

The new files are in place and are not in the patch (nothing compiles them until
app/build.py is patched): net2/session.h session_impl.h session.cpp session_mock.cpp
session_net.cpp carrier.h carrier.cpp hash.h cardchars.cpp clicmd.h clicmd.cpp game.h
hooks.h launch.cpp (and net2cli.cpp, play.sh, which the application does not link).

What the lines do, for whoever merges by hand:

  net/netdrv.cpp   (five places, nine lines; all of them do nothing in a program that does
                   not link net2/launch.cpp - the pointers of net2/hooks.h are then null)
    - include "../net2/hooks.h"
    - udp_send, udp_receive: first line - when net2 carries this UDP port of the game (a
      game started from a room: port 4000 while its launch and game run) the datagram goes
      to it and netdrv does nothing with it
    - per_blank: net2's turn once per vertical blank (its sockets)
    - fn_003226e4 (tasks_run): net2's turn before the pass (this is where it calls the
      game's own functions for the character screens and the launch) and after it (where
      it sees that the launch has completed, before the main loop loads the first level)

  app/build.py     seven more compile jobs for rta_app; no new HLE address, no translator
                   option: the translation is the one rta_app has today.

Not in this patch, on purpose: nothing in rt/, render/, app/app_main.cpp.  The stand-alone
program takes its room from runtime options (after "--"): --opt net2=host | join:CODE,
--opt net2-name=NAME, --opt net2-service=HOST:PORT ... (the head of net2/launch.cpp lists
them); net2/play.sh maps --play-host / --play-join CODE / --name NAME onto them.  If
app_main.cpp is to know those three itself, it is three lines in its option loop that push
the same "--opt" pairs onto the runtime's arguments.

A headless program for tests (tools/recomp/net/build_online.py) gets the same by adding
the seven sources as --extra files; not in the patch (tests here link a snapshot:
local/recomp-net2/build.sh).

No game patch: net2/proposed.pnach does not exist because nothing had to be patched.

